<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#groundnut">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Groundnut</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as groundnuts</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The groundnut, or peanut, is a legume crop originally from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> that became widely cultivated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>. In the nineteenth century it became an important export crop in the region. It features in the economies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaabu-empire">Kaabu Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a> as part of the shift towards agricultural exports.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Across the empire, the doctrine of mise en valeur framed colonial economies around producing raw materials for French industry: rubber from Indochina, phosphates from North Africa, <b>groundnuts</b> from Senegal, cotton from the Sahel.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-empire">French Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Agriculture formed the economic base, with rice and millet, and later <b>groundnuts</b>, cultivated across diverse ecological zones, while livestock rearing supplemented subsistence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kaabu-empire">Kaabu Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Cayor traded slaves, hides and <b>groundnuts</b> with the French at Gorée and Saint-Louis.</blockquote><a href="/state/cayor">Cayor</a></li><li><blockquote>Nigeria exported palm oil, cocoa, <b>groundnuts</b>, cotton and tin.</blockquote><a href="/state/colonial-nigeria">Colonial Nigeria</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Groundnuts</b> became the main cash crop, taxes and forced labour drove migration to Nigeria and Ghana, and education reached very few.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-niger">Colony of Niger</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Groundnuts</b> became the main export, and Sufi brotherhoods such as the Mourides shaped rural society.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-senegal">French Senegal</a></li></ul></section>
